<!-- ══ 9. OUTGROWN ══ -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="outgrown-h">
<div class="inner">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="tag tag-dim" style="margin-bottom:16px;display:inline-flex;">The ceiling</span>
    <h2 id="outgrown-h">When you've outgrown Sheets entirely</h2>
    <p>Three real signals, not a vague "eventually."</p>
  </div>
  <div class="body-text">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Approaching the cell ceiling.</strong> Google Sheets caps a spreadsheet at 10 million cells total across every tab (Google announced a 20-million-cell beta in April 2026, but 10M is the standing limit). A 9-column Orders tab hits that around 1.1 million rows.</li>
      <li><strong>Formula slowdown, well before the cell ceiling.</strong> SUMIF and VLOOKUP ranges recalculating across tens of thousands of rows visibly lag in the UI long before you're anywhere near 10 million cells — this is usually the first real symptom, not the row count itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Concurrent-write friction.</strong> Sheets was not built for high-frequency transactional writes from multiple sources. A busy store running order writes, inventory updates, and a reconciliation pass through the same spreadsheet can start to see write conflicts or queuing under sustained load.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When any of these show up consistently, the fix isn't a bigger spreadsheet — it's moving the system of record off Sheets. BigQuery is the natural next step if you're already in the Google ecosystem; a hosted Postgres instance or Airtable base works too. Keep Sheets as a lightweight view or export target on top, rather than the primary store, if you still want the formula-based reporting this guide is built around.</p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

